===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} Any of several [[hemiparasitic]] [[evergreen]] [[plant#Noun|plants]] of the [[order#Noun|order]] [[Santalales]] with [[white]] [[berry|berries]] that grow in the [[crown#Noun|crowns]] of [[apple tree]]s, [[oak]]s, and other [[tree]]s, such as the [[European mistletoe]] (''[[Viscum album]]'') and {{vern|American mistletoe}} or {{vern|eastern mistletoe}} ({{taxlink|Phoradendron leucarpum|species|noshow=1}}).
#* {{quote-journal|author=Olivia Judson|authorlink=Olivia Judson|title=The hemiparasite season [print version: Under the hemiparasite, ''International New York Times'', 24–25 December 2014, p.&nbsp;7]|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223181330/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archivedate=23 December 2014|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|date=23 December 2014|passage=[T]he stickiness [of mistletoe berries] persists through the gut, making it hard to defecate '''mistletoe''' seeds with any grace. To deal with this, birds like the [[w:silky-flycatcher|silky flycatcher]], Phainopepla nitens, that are '''mistletoe''' specialists, have evolved a "waggle dance"&nbsp;– also known as butt-wiping behavior&nbsp;– where they clean up by rubbing themselves against twigs. Indeed, the association with bird droppings may even be the origin of the word: Some evidence suggests that "'''mistletoe'''" comes from Anglo-Saxon for "dung on a twig."}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[sprig]] of one such plant used as a [[Christmas]] [[decoration]], associated with the [[custom#Noun|custom]] that a [[man#Noun|man]] may [[kiss#Verb|kiss]] any [[woman]] [[stand#Verb|standing]] beneath it.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|chapter=Stave Three. The Second of the Three Spirits.|pages=81–82|pageref=82|passage=There were ruddy, brown-faced, broad-girthed Spanish Onions, shining in the fatness of their growth like Spanish Friars; and winking from their shelves in wanton slyness at the girls as they went by, and glanced demurely at the hung-up '''mistletoe'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|author=[[w:Robert Wells (songwriter)|Robert Wells]]; {{w|Mel Tormé}}|title={{w|The Christmas Song}}|artist={{w|Nat King Cole}}|month=November|year=1946|passage=Everybody knows a turkey and some '''mistletoe''' / Help to make the season bright / Tiny tots with their eyes all aglow / Will find it hard to sleep tonight}}
#* {{quote-song|author=Johnny Marks|authorlink=Johnny Mark|title=[[w:Rockin' Around the Christmas Tree|Rockin’ Around the Christmas Tree]]|album=Merry Christmas from Brenda Lee|artist={{w|Brenda Lee}}|year=1958|passage=Rockin' around the Christmas tree / At the Christmas party hop / '''Mistletoe''' hung where you can see / Every couple tries to stop}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=Olivia Judson|authorlink=Olivia Judson|title=The hemiparasite season [print version: Under the hemiparasite, ''International New York Times'', 24–25 December 2014, p.&nbsp;7]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223181330/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/24/opinion/the-hemiparasite-season.html|archivedate=23 December 2014|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|date=23 December 2014|passage='''Mistletoe'''. The word conjures winter holidays, office parties, stolen kisses, romance. But appreciation of the plant is no modern thing. Kissing under the '''mistletoe''' has been happening for at least 200 years. And some 2,000 years ago, the druids in which is now Britain venerated the plant when it grew on an oak. When they found it, they dressed in white, harvested it with a golden sickle, and sacrificed two white bulls. Or so says that great Roman, {{w|Pliny the Elder}}, in his "[[w:Natural History (Pliny)|Natural History]]."}}

